00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.070
But the question is, why
does a company like Google

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:26.050
look at something like fishing?

00:04:26.050 --> 00:04:28.420
And so over a billion
people in the world

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:31.800
depend on fish as their
primary source of protein.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:35.050
About 90% of the apex,
or the largest species,

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:37.105
have disappeared
within our lifetime.

00:04:37.105 --> 00:04:40.280
85% of the world's fisheries are
either overfished, at capacity,

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.800
or are recovering.

00:04:41.800 --> 00:04:44.980
About one in five, or
20%, of the world's fish

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:46.300
are considered IEU--

00:04:46.300 --> 00:04:49.420
Illegal, Unregulated,
and Undocumented.

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:51.800
And then when we look at
this at a global level,

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.620
this is about a $60 billion
market that is really affecting

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:57.900
developing countries the most.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.900
And that ties down to
food security, [INAUDIBLE]

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:02.680
down to sovereign rights,
and a lot of other issues,

00:05:02.680 --> 00:05:05.680
like slave labor, that you
wouldn't think of initially.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.930
So let's talk a little bit
about how this is actually

00:05:07.930 --> 00:05:10.854
working behind the scenes.

00:05:10.854 --> 00:05:11.770
DAVID KROODMSA: Great.

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:13.090
Thank you, Brian.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:14.230
So I'm David Kroodsma.

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.940
I run the research program
at Global Fishing Watch.

00:05:16.940 --> 00:05:20.620
And Global Fishing Watch
is a small nonprofit

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:22.390
dedicated to
increasing transparency

00:05:22.390 --> 00:05:24.431
in the global fishing
industry, and a lot of what

00:05:24.431 --> 00:05:25.660
we do is using Google tools.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.590
And I'm going to go into details
of how that technology works,

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.980
how you make those really nice
maps that Brian just showed us.

00:05:32.980 --> 00:05:35.860
The key technology is something
called Automatic Identification

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:40.060
System, or AIS, that all
large, ocean-going vessels have

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:40.915
to carry.

00:05:40.915 --> 00:05:42.790
And what it does-- it
has a little GPS in it,

00:05:42.790 --> 00:05:44.498
and it broadcasts the
vessels' locations,

00:05:44.498 --> 00:05:46.450
so the other boats nearby
can see where it is

00:05:46.450 --> 00:05:47.635
and, thus, avoid collisions.

00:05:47.635 --> 00:05:49.310
It's a safety device.

00:05:49.310 --> 00:05:51.580
These signals, though,
are broadcast to a radio.

00:05:51.580 --> 00:05:55.260
And they can be picked up by
satellites flying overhead

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.965
or a network of antennas
along the world's coastline.

00:05:57.965 --> 00:05:59.840
And what we have done
at Global Fishing Watch

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.790
is obtained a global database
of all of these AIS messages

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:04.900
and then processed them.

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:08.080
If you plot all of these
messages on a map, what you see

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.450
is, over one year, something
that looks like this.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:12.820
This is one of my favorite maps.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:15.040
You can see all the
world's shipping lanes.

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:16.760
You can see where
fishing is happening.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:18.530
You can see places
near Antarctica

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:21.050
where boats don't go.

00:06:21.050 --> 00:06:22.070
This is all vessels.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.554
Only about one out of
five are fishing boats.

00:06:24.554 --> 00:06:25.970
So now, we have
this big database,

00:06:25.970 --> 00:06:28.880
and we have two
classification problems.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.452
One, we want to identify
which of the vessels

00:06:31.452 --> 00:06:33.660
are fishing vessels and what
type of fishing vessels.

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:35.660
And two, for those
vessels, we want

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:38.020
to identify when
they're fishing.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.530
And so this is where we use the
Cloud-- we use Google Cloud.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:43.280
First, let's start
with the first problem,

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:46.410
identifying what's a fishing
vessel and what type.

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.955
So in AIS messages, they
broadcast the name and call

00:06:49.955 --> 00:06:51.071
sign of a boat.

00:06:51.071 --> 00:06:52.820
And we've been able
to take these messages

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.890
and match them up to
official registries

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:59.030
to figure out what type of boat
about slightly more than 50,000

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:01.852
of these almost
300,000 vessels are.

00:07:01.852 --> 00:07:04.310
So that tells us, say, whether
it's a tugboat, whether it's

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:05.781
a trawler, and so forth.

00:07:05.781 --> 00:07:07.280
So we have this
large training data,

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:08.780
and then we have
to be able to infer

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:10.370
what the other vessels are.

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:13.534
Fortunately, boats have
very different patterns

00:07:13.534 --> 00:07:15.200
of how they move in
the ocean, depending

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:16.670
on what type of vessel it is.

00:07:16.670 --> 00:07:20.500
So here on the left,
you can see the paths

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:22.600
of a trawler, which is a
fishing vessel that drags

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.032
a net behind it to catch fish.

00:07:25.032 --> 00:07:26.990
In the middle is the path
of a longline, which,

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:29.392
as its name applies,
is-- sets long lines

00:07:29.392 --> 00:07:31.100
of hooks that can be
up to 100-kilometers

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:32.175
long in the ocean.

00:07:32.175 --> 00:07:33.800
And then on the right
is a purse seine,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.770
which encircles schools of
tuna or other fish with a net

00:07:36.770 --> 00:07:38.192
and draws them in that way.

00:07:38.192 --> 00:07:39.650
Each of them has
a distinct pattern

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:41.180
which you can recognize
with your eye, which

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.670
means we can probably train
a model to do the same thing

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:44.872
and differentiate them.

00:07:44.872 --> 00:07:47.330
But to train that model, first
we have to process this data

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:50.210
and create features for our
machine learning algorithm.

00:07:50.210 --> 00:07:53.780
And we do all that
in Google products.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:58.685
So every day, we get about 50
million unique AIS messages.

00:07:58.685 --> 00:08:00.570
And we run those messages.

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:03.355
We paralyze those
messages in Dataflow.

00:08:03.355 --> 00:08:05.480
And the first thing we do
is something very simple.

00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:08.180
We just create realistic
segments with every--

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:11.360
all the messages from these
vessels and get rid of noise

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:13.360
such that you have paths
that you can work with.

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:15.109
There's actually a lot
of noise in the AIS

00:08:15.109 --> 00:08:16.340
that needs to be cleaned up.

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:17.839
And then, we put
that into BigQuery.

00:08:17.839 --> 00:08:20.380
BigQuery is Google's platform--

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.290
it's an SQL-like
database that allows you

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:24.410
to do terabyte-sized queries.

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.820
It returns very
quickly, and it's

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.699
very useful for all
of our analyses.

00:08:28.699 --> 00:08:30.240
Now, we have all
these points, and we

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:32.323
want to figure out spatial
information about each,

00:08:32.323 --> 00:08:34.470
such as the distance
to shore, whether it's

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.559
in the exclusive economic
zone of China or Japan,

00:08:37.559 --> 00:08:38.520
distance to port.

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:42.654
And we do that by taking
global shape files,

00:08:42.654 --> 00:08:44.820
such as the one of all the
world's coastlines or one

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.780
of all the world's ports, and
do calculations in Dataflow

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:52.544
to find out for each
position in the world,

00:08:52.544 --> 00:08:54.960
that one-kilometer resolution--
so that's about 30 million

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:55.620
points--

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.080
the distance to the shore
and distance to port.

00:08:58.080 --> 00:09:01.139
We then take those rasters,
upload them to BigQuery.

00:09:01.139 --> 00:09:02.180
And then, it's very nice.

00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:03.480
We're able to do a
join, because that's

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.430
what you can do in BigQuery,
across these millions

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:07.804
and millions of positions to
get that spatial information

00:09:07.804 --> 00:09:09.995
about every single position.

00:09:09.995 --> 00:09:12.120
So now, we have a nice
useful data set in BigQuery.

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:14.580
And we want to create features
for our machine learning

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.080
algorithm.

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:19.710
We have about 30 billion
AIS messages in all.

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.560
This on the scale of a terabyte.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:26.115
And we continue, on Dataflow,
to do some calculations

00:09:26.115 --> 00:09:27.990
for each single position,
where you calculate

00:09:27.990 --> 00:09:29.910
11 different features.

00:09:29.910 --> 00:09:32.580
These are simple things, such
as the time between positions,

00:09:32.580 --> 00:09:34.470
the implied speed
between positions

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:37.020
of a vessel, how far
that is from shore,

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:44.460
how far that point is from an
anchorage, the time of day.

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:45.960
And so now, we have
some information

00:09:45.960 --> 00:09:48.450
about all these
features and we want

00:09:48.450 --> 00:09:50.850
to train a model to
identify what type of boat

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:53.070
it is based on these features.

00:09:53.070 --> 00:09:57.840
To do that, we use a common type
of machine learning algorithm

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.280
called the convolutional
neural network.

00:10:00.280 --> 00:10:01.380
This is a type of--

00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:03.360
this algorithm has
actually become very common

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:04.859
in image recognition
and is probably

00:10:04.859 --> 00:10:08.580
very similar to what Google
Photos uses to identify what's

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.800
a cat and what's not a cat.

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:13.210
And here's how it works.

00:10:13.210 --> 00:10:14.800
So we're taking--
for each vessel,

00:10:14.800 --> 00:10:16.980
say we have about
13,000 positions

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:20.820
we're going to try to use to
identify what type of boat

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:21.320
it is.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.520
And each of those
positions has 11 features,

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:27.615
so that's over 100,000
features per vessel.

00:10:27.615 --> 00:10:29.490
And we run something--
we run a convolution--

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:31.270
a nine-layer
convolution across it,

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:34.080
which essentially reduces
the number of features down

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:37.200
to something more manageable--
in this case, about 2,000--

00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.350
which we can then run across
a fully-connected network

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:43.660
to get 14 different vessel
class probabilities.

00:10:43.660 --> 00:10:45.825
So the output of
this is a number

00:10:45.825 --> 00:10:47.950
that tells you whether or
not the neural net thinks

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:49.545
the boat is a
tugboat, whether it

00:10:49.545 --> 00:10:52.680
thinks it's a trawler, whether
it thinks it's a longliner.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.710
This next slide
shows how well we

00:10:55.710 --> 00:10:58.560
think something called a
confusion matrix, which

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:00.840
is a common tool
used for identifying

00:11:00.840 --> 00:11:05.340
how well machine learning
algorithms do at classification

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:05.994
problems.

00:11:05.994 --> 00:11:07.410
It's a little
complicated to read.

00:11:07.410 --> 00:11:11.880
But basically, one row of
this is all the vessels

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:14.370
from one class in our test data.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.540
And the column is how many
of the boats from that class

00:11:18.540 --> 00:11:21.130
the neural net thought
was in that class.

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:24.170
So basically, high numbers
along the diagonal is good.

00:11:24.170 --> 00:11:25.920
That means that vessels
from the test data

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:28.550
were labeled with the
same value in the--

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:29.760
by the neural network.

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.550
And as you look at
this, it gets almost--

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:37.210
has a very high accuracy
on our data set.

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.172
A lot of the vessels it gets
wrong, I've looked at them.

00:11:40.172 --> 00:11:41.880
Oftentimes, they're
very inactive vessels

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.285
or they're some vessels which
have kind of mixed class

00:11:44.285 --> 00:11:47.410
or outside of these 14 classes.

00:11:47.410 --> 00:11:49.800
So we do a good job identifying
what's a fishing boat

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:53.110
and what's not and the
type of fishing vessel.

00:11:53.110 --> 00:11:55.860
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So how many
boats are you looking at here?

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.276
DAVID KROODMSA:
We're looking at--

00:11:57.276 --> 00:11:59.670
so there's 300,000
vessels in the world.

00:11:59.670 --> 00:12:01.266
In one year and
across all years,

00:12:01.266 --> 00:12:03.390
it's actually closer to
400,000, because some boats

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:04.600
aren't active every year.

00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:08.340
And there's about
60,000 fishing vessels

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:10.999
active in one year and about
70,000 active across all years.

00:12:10.999 --> 00:12:11.790
BRIAN SULLIVAN: OK.

00:12:11.790 --> 00:12:13.519
So it's about
70,000 vessels that

00:12:13.519 --> 00:12:14.810
are being shown in the public--

00:12:14.810 --> 00:12:15.590
DAVID KROODMSA: Yes.

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:16.110
BRIAN SULLIVAN:
--Global Fishing Watch.

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:16.530
DAVID KROODMSA: Yes.

00:12:16.530 --> 00:12:18.810
BRIAN SULLIVAN: And then
there's the full 300,000

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:20.751
that we're doing our
own analytics on inside.

00:12:20.751 --> 00:12:21.750
DAVID KROODMSA: Exactly.

00:12:21.750 --> 00:12:22.920
No, that's a good
point, because when

00:12:22.920 --> 00:12:23.670
you look at Global
Fishing Watch,

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:24.870
you're just seeing
the fishing vessels.

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:26.310
But in order to
identify them, we

00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:28.151
have to process all the vessels.

00:12:28.151 --> 00:12:28.942
BRIAN SULLIVAN: OK.

00:12:31.645 --> 00:12:34.020
DAVID KROODMSA: So that then
brings us-- so now we know--

00:12:34.020 --> 00:12:36.180
oh, the other thing I
think is really exciting

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:38.940
about this is that big
boats move differently

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:39.820
than small boats.

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:43.170
So our model can also tell
how large the vessels are.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:49.320
This is the length, engine
power, and tonnage of--

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:52.710
on the x-axis here is
from our test data.

00:12:52.710 --> 00:12:54.877
And the y-axis is what
the model predicts.

00:12:54.877 --> 00:12:57.460
And it does a really good job
of predicting how big boats are,

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:59.335
which can be useful for
all types of studies.

00:12:59.335 --> 00:13:01.043
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So
what you're doing here

00:13:01.043 --> 00:13:03.750
is you're taking the AIS,
which is kind of like a GPS

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:07.260
track of the boat, and the
only information you have

00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:10.800
is how fast it's
moving and when we're

00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.276
seeing a signal from that.

00:13:12.276 --> 00:13:13.650
And with that
information, you're

00:13:13.650 --> 00:13:16.847
figuring out how big of a boat
it is, how much power it has,

00:13:16.847 --> 00:13:18.930
and then just like how
much it's weighing as well?

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:19.180
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah.

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:21.270
It's kind of-- I think
it's kind of amazing.

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:23.052
But that's exactly
what we're doing.

00:13:23.052 --> 00:13:24.510
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So
it's like Strava

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:25.530
for all the world's ships.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.154
DAVID KROODMSA:
[LAUGHS] Yeah, exactly.

00:13:27.154 --> 00:13:29.560
Essentially.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:31.080
Yeah, and it can tell what's--

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:33.420
you know, and it's--

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:36.500
it's amazing how much
you can tell just

00:13:36.500 --> 00:13:37.840
based on how it moves.

00:13:37.840 --> 00:13:38.732
BRIAN SULLIVAN: OK.

00:13:38.732 --> 00:13:41.070
DAVID KROODMSA: Which is
the next thing, which is you

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:42.670
want to know when it's fishing.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:43.800
So now, we know what
type of boat it is.

00:13:43.800 --> 00:13:45.466
And you want to look
at how it's moving.

00:13:45.466 --> 00:13:47.580
And you want to say,
OK, this is a trawler.

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:49.800
And right now, we think it
has its net in the water.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:51.720
Or this time-- or you want
to say, this is a trawler.

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:53.130
Right now, it doesn't
have its net the water,

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.088
and it's going to port.

00:13:54.088 --> 00:13:56.490
Because you want to say
where fishing is happening.

00:13:56.490 --> 00:13:58.230
And the way we do
that is we need--

00:13:58.230 --> 00:14:02.430
just like with the
vessel classification,

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:03.460
we need label data.

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:06.120
So we need to know
where fishing--

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:07.370
we need to take all the data--

00:14:07.370 --> 00:14:09.495
we need to take, basically,
all the tracks of boats

00:14:09.495 --> 00:14:11.640
and label each position
as fishing or non-fishing.

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:12.574
BRIAN SULLIVAN: OK.

00:14:12.574 --> 00:14:14.740
DAVID KROODMSA: This turned
out to be a lot of work.

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:17.910
[LAUGHS] We had
to manually label

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:19.865
more than a million points.

00:14:19.865 --> 00:14:21.990
This is a case where grad
students are your friend.

00:14:21.990 --> 00:14:24.300
We partnered with a number
of research institutions

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:26.100
that helped us with this.

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:29.340
And we built up a
JavaScript tool--

00:14:29.340 --> 00:14:31.260
you can see a
screenshot of it here--

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:33.434
in something called PYBOSSA.

00:14:33.434 --> 00:14:35.850
And you can label-- what we
do is you look at these tracks

00:14:35.850 --> 00:14:36.891
of the boat and we were--

00:14:36.891 --> 00:14:38.820
fisheries experts
helped train us.

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:42.340
And you label each position
as fishing or non-fishing.

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:44.490
We labeled more than
a million positions.

00:14:44.490 --> 00:14:47.700
The way we did this largely
also was me every day

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:49.860
bothering people
on Slack and then

00:14:49.860 --> 00:14:52.650
sending out encouraging
notes or critical ones

00:14:52.650 --> 00:14:54.250
if they were not
labeling enough.

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:56.670
We did that for two months.

00:14:56.670 --> 00:14:58.652
And then, we were able
to train our model.

00:14:58.652 --> 00:15:00.360
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So at
Google, there's a--

00:15:00.360 --> 00:15:02.670
machine learning is something
that's coming up all the time.

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:04.086
And usually whenever
someone wants

00:15:04.086 --> 00:15:06.300
to apply machine
learning to a project,

00:15:06.300 --> 00:15:08.600
the biggest issue is
getting training data.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:11.090
And so how long does
it take you to amass

00:15:11.090 --> 00:15:12.330
a million points like this?

00:15:12.330 --> 00:15:15.390
DAVID KROODMSA: So it was--
we were helped out a lot by--

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:18.180
the grad student had basically
spent a year doing this.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:19.900
So that was quite helpful.

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:23.250
Thank you, Christina,
if you're listening.

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:26.610
And then, it took two months
to get another few hundred--

00:15:26.610 --> 00:15:28.010
basically, she did half of it.

00:15:28.010 --> 00:15:30.900
And then, it took us two months
to get another half of that

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:34.200
through me bothering
everyone on Slack

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:38.169
and a team of about 10
people spending each

00:15:38.169 --> 00:15:39.210
some time during the day.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:40.530
So we calculated
that it would take--

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:42.590
if we were to do this
across the whole data set--

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:43.590
it would take us about--

00:15:43.590 --> 00:15:46.620
I think it was like 400 years to
label all the fishing activity.

00:15:46.620 --> 00:15:48.120
BRIAN SULLIVAN: Oh,
so you're saying

00:15:48.120 --> 00:15:50.190
that if a human were to
do it at the same speed

00:15:50.190 --> 00:15:52.950
that a grad student would
do it, that grad student

00:15:52.950 --> 00:15:54.085
would be worth 400 years.

00:15:54.085 --> 00:15:54.960
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah.

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:56.280
And one of the tricky
things here is we

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:57.960
couldn't-- there's a
lot of nice tools--

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.376
I know Google's been developing
these to kind of outsource

00:16:00.376 --> 00:16:03.570
these labeling projects
for creating label data.

00:16:03.570 --> 00:16:05.760
But we actually had to
be trained to do this,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.860
because we needed to
have kind of expert

00:16:07.860 --> 00:16:09.700
decisions about what's
fishing versus not.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:12.284
So we couldn't outsource it in
the way we might have liked to.

00:16:12.284 --> 00:16:13.866
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So
it's a nice example

00:16:13.866 --> 00:16:16.830
for machine learning where like
if a human looks at the track,

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:19.590
they kind of have an intuitive
sense of why it's fishing

00:16:19.590 --> 00:16:20.620
or it's not fishing.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:22.770
But there's not a
clear formula that you

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:24.100
can run the same way on.

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:26.610
DAVID KROODMSA: And there is,
to some extent, by gear type.

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.477
But all those rules
are complicated enough

00:16:29.477 --> 00:16:31.560
that it's better to have
human judgment put on it.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:39.200
And so the output of it
is a table like this.

00:16:39.200 --> 00:16:41.570
This is a view of
what's in BigQuery.

00:16:41.570 --> 00:16:43.700
And the far right
column is the--

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:45.800
NNS, Neural Net Score.

00:16:45.800 --> 00:16:48.200
And it's one if we think the
boat is fishing and zero,

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:49.324
or null, if we don't think.

00:16:49.324 --> 00:16:51.860
So now, we have every single
one of our data points labeled

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:53.870
as fishing or not fishing.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:57.380
And we also think we know what
types of vessels they are.

00:16:57.380 --> 00:17:01.220
So now what we can do is
a-- now, we can do analyses.

00:17:01.220 --> 00:17:03.620
Now, we can tell
stories and make maps.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:07.500
One thing I do almost every day
is write queries in BigQuery

00:17:07.500 --> 00:17:10.099
and then also link up
to that using Python.

00:17:10.099 --> 00:17:13.660
This is a screenshot
of an IPython notebook

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:17.480
where I have a query that is
trying to group the group's

00:17:17.480 --> 00:17:20.510
data from a BigQuery table
into latitude and longitude

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:22.550
to make a grid.

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:24.270
I use [INAUDIBLE] library.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:25.329
It has a nice--

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:28.970
it has a module for
connecting up with BigQuery.

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:30.740
I take that out into
a raster, and then I

00:17:30.740 --> 00:17:33.120
can map it and do analyses.

00:17:33.120 --> 00:17:37.220
And so here is a global map
of just the fishing vessels

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:41.150
while we think they are
fishing in one calendar year.

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:43.517
And this is basically
a scientific product,

00:17:43.517 --> 00:17:45.350
the same thing that
Brian showed you earlier

00:17:45.350 --> 00:17:49.340
and what we're showing
on that global map.

00:17:49.340 --> 00:17:51.560
If I changed the way I write
that query to query only

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:53.596
by specific gear
types, I get a map

00:17:53.596 --> 00:17:55.220
of where all trawlers
in the world are.

00:17:55.220 --> 00:17:57.890
And you can see they fish
along the world's coastlines.

00:17:57.890 --> 00:18:00.360
They often drag their
nets along the sea floor,

00:18:00.360 --> 00:18:02.630
so they stay close
to the shore--

00:18:02.630 --> 00:18:05.390
drifting longlines, which set
those hooks 100-kilometers long

00:18:05.390 --> 00:18:10.810
out in the high seas; purse
seines, which encircle fish

00:18:10.810 --> 00:18:11.880
with their nets.

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:13.990
And so you can see
each fishing pattern

00:18:13.990 --> 00:18:16.830
has a distinct
location in the world.

00:18:16.830 --> 00:18:25.120
So in summary, we are
taking in an AIS feed

00:18:25.120 --> 00:18:26.850
from over 30
satellites and hundreds

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:31.920
of terrestrial receivers
with almost 50 million AIS

00:18:31.920 --> 00:18:36.150
messages per day from 300,000
vessels across a year over six

00:18:36.150 --> 00:18:37.627
years of data.

00:18:37.627 --> 00:18:39.210
We then, also, for
the training, we're

00:18:39.210 --> 00:18:41.760
using vessel registries to
identify the types of boats

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:45.330
and also the over 20,000
manually-labeled track segments

00:18:45.330 --> 00:18:47.040
or a million positions.

00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:50.970
We put that all together through
the various Google tools.

00:18:50.970 --> 00:18:55.560
And what we get out is about
70,000 fishing vessels,

00:18:55.560 --> 00:18:57.180
14 gear types--

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:59.130
14 vessel types
of which half are

00:18:59.130 --> 00:19:03.240
fishing types, the
size of vessels,

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.540
when we think they're fishing,
and all of these wonderful maps

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:08.062
and data that you see available.

00:19:08.062 --> 00:19:10.520
One thing I should add is that
we're trying to make as much

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.260
of the data publicly available,
as well as our code base,

00:19:13.260 --> 00:19:17.340
so that our machine learning
algorithms, which we also

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:21.840
run through TensorFlow and
Cloud ML, are our open source.

00:19:21.840 --> 00:19:24.390
And you can go to
globalfishingwatch.io

00:19:24.390 --> 00:19:26.910
and find links to
those GitHub reposts

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:28.500
to see how we actually did this.

00:19:31.434 --> 00:19:33.100
And again, it would
take about 400 years

00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:35.705
for me bothering you on
Slack to complete it.

00:19:40.300 --> 00:19:43.220
We used Google products at
almost every step of the way.

00:19:43.220 --> 00:19:46.810
We use Dataflow, which is
really nice for scaling up

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:48.580
Compute Engine
instances, so we don't

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:50.740
have to worry about allocating
all of our resources

00:19:50.740 --> 00:19:53.940
or keeping too many
machines running.

00:19:53.940 --> 00:19:56.560
BigQuery is how we store a lot
of our data and run queries

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:57.060
on it.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:59.160
I use it every day.

00:19:59.160 --> 00:20:05.020
Cloud ML is-- and TensorFlow
we use for our models, Cloud

00:20:05.020 --> 00:20:06.910
Storage as well.

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:10.446
Apps Engine is used on the
map, as well as Maps API,

00:20:10.446 --> 00:20:12.820
for showing off the interactive
map that Brian showed you

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:13.320
earlier.

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:14.861
And then, I'll talk
a little bit more

00:20:14.861 --> 00:20:17.200
about Earth Engine, which
is another platform used

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:17.920
for analysis.

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:19.420
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So
one of the things

00:20:19.420 --> 00:20:22.120
about Global Fishing Watch
is it's a partnership now

00:20:22.120 --> 00:20:24.490
between Google, that
was a founding partner,

00:20:24.490 --> 00:20:26.750
and, initially, two other
non-profits, SkyTruth

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:27.700
and Oceana.

00:20:27.700 --> 00:20:31.180
And now, we've stood it up as
its own independent nonprofit.

00:20:31.180 --> 00:20:33.430
So it's been a great
opportunity as these projects

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:34.966
were evolving.

00:20:34.966 --> 00:20:37.340
When we were in the early
stages of Global Fishing Watch,

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:39.130
Dataflow was just coming out.

00:20:39.130 --> 00:20:42.160
So being able to give external
people alpha access to this

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:45.460
to really refine what were
the features that we needed,

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:48.590
what worked well, and what
didn't, has been really fun.

00:20:48.590 --> 00:20:50.440
And so I know BigQuery
is one that we

00:20:50.440 --> 00:20:52.790
have a whole team
of data scientists

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:55.420
that don't really have
the background in systems

00:20:55.420 --> 00:20:57.040
administration
and really scaling

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:59.279
massive parallel computing.

00:20:59.279 --> 00:21:01.070
But by being able to
do everything directly

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:03.370
in BigQuery, they were
comfortable in SQL.

00:21:03.370 --> 00:21:06.130
And suddenly, they were
doing these massive analyses

00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:08.240
that they wouldn't have
been able to do otherwise.

00:21:08.240 --> 00:21:10.090
DAVID KROODMSA: And that's
kind of a lot of where I sit,

00:21:10.090 --> 00:21:10.590
actually.

00:21:10.590 --> 00:21:13.510
Because I'm doing a
lot of the analyses

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:16.060
and working with our engineers
who are doing some of the more

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:16.950
distributive stuff.

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:19.366
And it's actually been kind
of fun over the last few years

00:21:19.366 --> 00:21:21.959
to see us kind of move over to
these new platforms which are

00:21:21.959 --> 00:21:24.250
auto-scaling and which are
kind of doing the same thing

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:27.010
for BigQuery-- that BigQuery's
done for me for some of these,

00:21:27.010 --> 00:21:30.640
like the Cloud ML
and the compute--

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:33.257
and the Dataflow.

00:21:33.257 --> 00:21:34.840
Because at the end
of the day, I don't

00:21:34.840 --> 00:21:37.869
want to worry about how to scale
up all these compute instances

00:21:37.869 --> 00:21:38.410
and all that.

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:40.120
That's why BigQuery is
so nice, because I never

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.010
have to think about
the parallelization.

00:21:42.010 --> 00:21:43.010
It just does it.

00:21:47.075 --> 00:21:48.701
And that bring us
back to the analysis,

00:21:48.701 --> 00:21:50.575
because really, what we
care about is getting

00:21:50.575 --> 00:21:51.910
useful information out of this.

00:21:51.910 --> 00:21:53.740
And so a large part
of my job is working

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.589
with 11 different research
institutions in our research

00:21:56.589 --> 00:21:58.130
program to take this
massive data set

00:21:58.130 --> 00:22:00.130
and turn it into
useful knowledge.

00:22:00.130 --> 00:22:02.920
And we're doing that through
over 25 different research

00:22:02.920 --> 00:22:04.180
projects.

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:05.800
We have 10 papers
already published;

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:07.309
many, many more in the works.

00:22:07.309 --> 00:22:09.100
And I'm gonna give you
two examples of what

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.110
we can do with this.

00:22:11.110 --> 00:22:13.564
One is just simply
people are trying

00:22:13.564 --> 00:22:14.980
to understand how
fishing responds

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:16.310
to different temperatures.

00:22:16.310 --> 00:22:17.590
So what you're looking
at here is an animation.

00:22:17.590 --> 00:22:19.720
The background shows
sea surface temperature,

00:22:19.720 --> 00:22:23.099
and then the dark blue is
drifting longline fishing

00:22:23.099 --> 00:22:24.640
activity over the
course of one year.

00:22:24.640 --> 00:22:27.075
And you can watch the
fishing activity move,

00:22:27.075 --> 00:22:29.209
and you can watch the
temperatures change.

00:22:29.209 --> 00:22:31.000
Some of our researchers
are building models

00:22:31.000 --> 00:22:33.339
to see how fishing responds
to changing temperatures.

00:22:33.339 --> 00:22:35.380
BRIAN SULLIVAN: Just to
make sure we understand--

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:38.920
you're saying that
near the equator,

00:22:38.920 --> 00:22:41.380
the water is a lot hotter, and
so you can actually see like

00:22:41.380 --> 00:22:44.260
little jet streams and things
like this coming off of-- that

00:22:44.260 --> 00:22:46.175
may not be the right
scientific term, but--

00:22:46.175 --> 00:22:47.080
DAVID KROODMSA:
Yeah, yeah, it works.

00:22:47.080 --> 00:22:48.730
BRIAN SULLIVAN: But you can see
currents coming through here

00:22:48.730 --> 00:22:50.416
and how those
temperatures are shifting.

00:22:50.416 --> 00:22:52.040
And all the wiggling
dots in the middle

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:53.505
is where they're using longline.

00:22:53.505 --> 00:22:55.630
And then down here, this
would be much colder water

00:22:55.630 --> 00:22:56.530
near the poles.

00:22:56.530 --> 00:22:57.650
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah.

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:01.810
And I'm going to zoom in now to
between Australia and Africa.

00:23:01.810 --> 00:23:04.600
The colors will change.

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:07.600
Yellow is very warm, black is
cold, and the white fishing

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:08.640
activity.

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.114
And this is between May
and September of 2016.

00:23:11.114 --> 00:23:13.030
And you can watch and
see the fishing activity

00:23:13.030 --> 00:23:15.790
follow this kind of
constant temperature

00:23:15.790 --> 00:23:17.650
as the gradient moves northward.

00:23:17.650 --> 00:23:19.390
And it's really
cool, and we've been

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:21.640
able look at how this
changes between years.

00:23:21.640 --> 00:23:23.460
And we can now model this.

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:25.347
And this is useful for
all types of things,

00:23:25.347 --> 00:23:27.055
ranging from how we
manage the oceans now

00:23:27.055 --> 00:23:28.971
and thinking about where
fishing is happening,

00:23:28.971 --> 00:23:30.880
how to allocate permits
to thinking about how

00:23:30.880 --> 00:23:32.600
to respond to climate change.

00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:34.680
And we built this--

00:23:34.680 --> 00:23:36.190
this graphic you're
seeing here was

00:23:36.190 --> 00:23:37.780
built using Earth
Engine, which is

00:23:37.780 --> 00:23:42.490
a Google platform for doing
very large global raster

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:43.730
calculations.

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:46.600
And that's with the sea surface
temperature model built into it

00:23:46.600 --> 00:23:48.730
at very high resolution
daily, that we're

00:23:48.730 --> 00:23:51.290
able to compare with
our fishing activity.

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:54.190
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So we're
seeing as the waters get warmer

00:23:54.190 --> 00:23:56.440
and move north,
you're saying the fish

00:23:56.440 --> 00:23:58.060
follow that same temperature.

00:23:58.060 --> 00:23:58.420
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah.

00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:00.211
BRIAN SULLIVAN: And
then the fishing boats,

00:24:00.211 --> 00:24:02.720
or the predators, are
following them the same way.

00:24:02.720 --> 00:24:04.386
And I think there's
actually researchers

00:24:04.386 --> 00:24:06.940
that are looking at, like, what
would be the migration paths

00:24:06.940 --> 00:24:10.720
for different fish
and other species

00:24:10.720 --> 00:24:13.570
like that as we have sea
surface temperatures rising

00:24:13.570 --> 00:24:15.670
across the board and then,
how will other ones be

00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:16.600
squeezed in and out?

00:24:16.600 --> 00:24:18.310
And so this is giving
new tools for them

00:24:18.310 --> 00:24:19.810
to look at that,
that weren't really

00:24:19.810 --> 00:24:22.865
possible or available before.

00:24:22.865 --> 00:24:25.240
DAVID KROODMSA: A second
project that's good to highlight

00:24:25.240 --> 00:24:27.010
is something called
transshipment.

00:24:27.010 --> 00:24:29.740
So some vessels, instead
of returning to port

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:31.570
and offloading
their fish at port,

00:24:31.570 --> 00:24:34.880
they meet up with refrigerated
cargo vessels at sea

00:24:34.880 --> 00:24:36.520
to offload their fish.

00:24:36.520 --> 00:24:39.370
And they do this partially
because sometimes, it

00:24:39.370 --> 00:24:40.540
can save fuel.

00:24:40.540 --> 00:24:43.570
But another reason is that
it can also help obscure

00:24:43.570 --> 00:24:46.460
where the fish was caught and
what vessel caught the fish.

00:24:46.460 --> 00:24:49.480
And this is one way that
illegally-caught fish enters

00:24:49.480 --> 00:24:51.020
legitimate markets.

00:24:51.020 --> 00:24:54.970
It also allows vessels to stay
at sea for a very long time

00:24:54.970 --> 00:24:58.300
without coming into port, which
has been shown to be associated

00:24:58.300 --> 00:25:01.660
with instances of
essentially slavery at sea

00:25:01.660 --> 00:25:04.480
and also other
illicit activities.

00:25:04.480 --> 00:25:07.110
And so our essential goal
is to create transparency.

00:25:07.110 --> 00:25:09.355
Not all of this
transshipment is bad.

00:25:09.355 --> 00:25:11.230
But where our goal is
to create transparency,

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:12.855
we can understand
where it's happening.

00:25:12.855 --> 00:25:16.885
And so we can better
assess its activity.

00:25:16.885 --> 00:25:19.177
If you could imagine,
this is basically--

00:25:19.177 --> 00:25:21.260
we have all the positions
of all the boats in AIS,

00:25:21.260 --> 00:25:22.968
and we want to see
when two boats meet up

00:25:22.968 --> 00:25:26.390
at sea, which is basically
a cross-join between tens

00:25:26.390 --> 00:25:29.450
of millions of positions, which
is an enormous calculation.

00:25:29.450 --> 00:25:31.820
We do this in Dataflow
by gridding the world up

00:25:31.820 --> 00:25:33.890
and breaking it down
into smaller pieces,

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:37.850
so we can identify everywhere
one fishing vessel meets up

00:25:37.850 --> 00:25:42.496
with a refrigerated cargo
vessel at sea for several hours.

00:25:42.496 --> 00:25:44.870
And from that, we're able to
create the first ever global

00:25:44.870 --> 00:25:47.040
map of transshipment behavior.

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:48.750
So this shows the--
the red dots here

00:25:48.750 --> 00:25:52.430
show everywhere we see a
refrigerated cargo vessel

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:56.570
and a fishing vessel
meet up at sea.

00:25:56.570 --> 00:25:59.750
And the light blue
dots are places see--

00:25:59.750 --> 00:26:02.000
where we think transshipment
might be occurring,

00:26:02.000 --> 00:26:02.876
with less confidence.

00:26:02.876 --> 00:26:04.416
BRIAN SULLIVAN: So
what you're saying

00:26:04.416 --> 00:26:06.710
is if you look in these
photos, this smaller boat

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:08.870
is the actual fishing vessel.

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:12.830
And then that pulls alongside
the larger refrigerated vessel,

00:26:12.830 --> 00:26:15.380
or like a tanker, and then
they offload everything

00:26:15.380 --> 00:26:17.560
from the small boat
onto the big boat.

00:26:17.560 --> 00:26:19.850
And so the big boat may
not actually be fishing,

00:26:19.850 --> 00:26:22.910
but it meets up with all these
small boats around the area

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:25.076
and then brings that back
into a central place.

00:26:25.076 --> 00:26:25.950
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah.

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:27.000
And the reason you
want to do this

00:26:27.000 --> 00:26:28.910
is because big boats are
more efficient at crossing

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:30.035
the ocean than small boats.

00:26:30.035 --> 00:26:32.060
And so you can save
fuel if you do this.

00:26:32.060 --> 00:26:35.754
But again, it also
obscures supply chains.

00:26:35.754 --> 00:26:37.670
And so Brian's going to
talk a little bit more

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:39.280
about why this matters.

00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:41.280
BRIAN SULLIVAN: Yup.

00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:44.220
So one of the other impacts that
we've seen of transshipments

00:26:44.220 --> 00:26:47.910
is that the same way they'll
be moving fish from a fishing

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:50.400
vessel onto a cargo
vessel, they'll

00:26:50.400 --> 00:26:52.710
also bring supplies and all
kinds of other materials

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:54.690
back onto the fishing
vessel, so the fishing

00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:57.930
vessel could stay out for
months and months at a time.

00:26:57.930 --> 00:27:00.930
And the AP did some incredible
investigative reporting.

00:27:00.930 --> 00:27:03.180
And then what they found is
that in a lot these cases,

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:05.910
the crew is actually
related to slave labor,

00:27:05.910 --> 00:27:08.060
because they're literally
trapped on the boat

00:27:08.060 --> 00:27:09.660
and unable to leave.

00:27:09.660 --> 00:27:11.430
And so being able
to help map out

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:13.410
where transshipments
were happening

00:27:13.410 --> 00:27:16.380
was a key piece for
helping them to understand

00:27:16.380 --> 00:27:18.180
where these vessels
might be going

00:27:18.180 --> 00:27:19.974
and how these
supplies are going.

00:27:19.974 --> 00:27:21.390
DAVID KROODMSA:
And those pictures

00:27:21.390 --> 00:27:23.850
you saw earlier of those
transshipments vessels,

00:27:23.850 --> 00:27:27.660
those were actually taken from
satellites working with members

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:29.900
of Global Fishing
Watch who are looking

00:27:29.900 --> 00:27:33.060
at AIS data of those large
cargo vessels and saying,

00:27:33.060 --> 00:27:34.570
we think there's a boat here.

00:27:34.570 --> 00:27:36.780
Why don't you tell your
satellite to take a picture?

00:27:36.780 --> 00:27:38.294
So it's-- yeah.

00:27:38.294 --> 00:27:39.960
BRIAN SULLIVAN: And
that's moved on to--

00:27:39.960 --> 00:27:41.085
I think the next slide is--

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:48.500
here's another example
where in Palau, people

00:27:48.500 --> 00:27:50.750
were looking at
poaching that was

00:27:50.750 --> 00:27:53.570
happening within a small
country in the Asia-Pacific.

00:27:53.570 --> 00:27:57.050
And in many cases, being able
to see this as it was happening,

00:27:57.050 --> 00:28:00.170
they were able to send
out enforcement vehicles

00:28:00.170 --> 00:28:02.150
from the government to
pull alongside these.

00:28:02.150 --> 00:28:03.650
And "The New York
Times Magazine"

00:28:03.650 --> 00:28:05.612
did a whole piece
on the story of how

00:28:05.612 --> 00:28:07.070
a lot of these
developing countries

00:28:07.070 --> 00:28:10.730
are being taken advantage of by
more developed and industrial

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:13.750
fishing countries and
companies to exploit this.

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:17.270
And so adding transparency to
a space that was traditionally

00:28:17.270 --> 00:28:20.960
out of sight and out of mind is
really revolutionizing the way

00:28:20.960 --> 00:28:24.820
people can more sustainably
manage these areas.

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:27.430
Indonesia is a
country that we've

00:28:27.430 --> 00:28:28.720
been working closely with.

00:28:28.720 --> 00:28:30.960
The person here in this
picture is Ibu Susi.

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:33.730
She's the minister of
fisheries and foreign affairs.

00:28:33.730 --> 00:28:36.340
She reports directly
to President Jokowi.

00:28:36.340 --> 00:28:38.119
And shortly after
we first announced

00:28:38.119 --> 00:28:39.910
that we're working on
Global Fishing Watch,

00:28:39.910 --> 00:28:41.090
she came to Google.

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:42.520
And she met with
us, and she said,

00:28:42.520 --> 00:28:45.850
how do I use technology
like this in Indonesia?

00:28:45.850 --> 00:28:48.280
And so she's been
extremely progressive.

00:28:48.280 --> 00:28:50.290
And she is the
first country ever

00:28:50.290 --> 00:28:53.410
to take what was traditionally
very proprietary data--

00:28:53.410 --> 00:28:56.200
so she's supplementing
our AIS work

00:28:56.200 --> 00:28:59.770
with what we call VMS, or
Vessel Monitoring Systems,

00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:02.500
which is proprietary
government data.

00:29:02.500 --> 00:29:05.320
And what they found is that
while a lot of these countries

00:29:05.320 --> 00:29:08.409
and companies have more
data than they can handle,

00:29:08.409 --> 00:29:09.950
the challenge isn't
the lack of data.

00:29:09.950 --> 00:29:12.330
The challenge is pulling
out insights from them.

00:29:12.330 --> 00:29:14.410
And so by taking her
data and running it

00:29:14.410 --> 00:29:16.570
through all of our
machine learning programs,

00:29:16.570 --> 00:29:19.690
we've been able to give her
back a lot of the other insights

00:29:19.690 --> 00:29:23.910
and let her and her teams
and her navy have more views

00:29:23.910 --> 00:29:25.540
into what's actually
happening, so they

00:29:25.540 --> 00:29:27.970
can focus on specific
things instead of swimming

00:29:27.970 --> 00:29:31.170
in all of that data.

00:29:31.170 --> 00:29:32.940
When we talked about
transshipments,

00:29:32.940 --> 00:29:35.700
it really does enable
a scale of fishing

00:29:35.700 --> 00:29:37.760
that was never possible before.

00:29:37.760 --> 00:29:39.900
And so this is a
very sobering picture

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.859
in the hull of a boat
in the Galapagos.

00:29:42.859 --> 00:29:45.150
And you're literally looking
at thousands and thousands

00:29:45.150 --> 00:29:48.420
of sharks that have
been finned and cut.

00:29:48.420 --> 00:29:52.200
And what ended up happening as
we zoom out, as you can see,

00:29:52.200 --> 00:29:54.270
how they've been removed
in different areas.

00:29:54.270 --> 00:29:56.270
These are hammerhead
sharks and silky sharks.

00:29:56.270 --> 00:29:58.820
There are some tuna
in there as well.

00:29:58.820 --> 00:30:03.490
And so working with National
Geographic, as well as many

00:30:03.490 --> 00:30:05.980
of the Galapagos
conservative groups,

00:30:05.980 --> 00:30:07.532
they were able to go in.

00:30:07.532 --> 00:30:09.490
And they said that these
were thousands, if not

00:30:09.490 --> 00:30:11.050
tens of thousands of sharks.

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:11.980
This is historic.

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:14.380
This is the biggest seizure
of sharks in the history

00:30:14.380 --> 00:30:16.700
of Galapagos, for sure.

00:30:16.700 --> 00:30:20.330
And what happened in the end
is the boat that they caught

00:30:20.330 --> 00:30:25.020
was fined about $6 million, and
the crew is currently in jail.

00:30:25.020 --> 00:30:27.590
And the Ecuadorian Navy
did an incredible job

00:30:27.590 --> 00:30:29.420
of bringing that in
and then trying them

00:30:29.420 --> 00:30:30.830
in their court systems.

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:33.680
But what they tried them for
is somewhat of a loophole,

00:30:33.680 --> 00:30:36.800
because they were transporting
endangered species

00:30:36.800 --> 00:30:39.730
through a marine protected area.

00:30:39.730 --> 00:30:42.300
But what Global Fishing Watch
is allowing people to do

00:30:42.300 --> 00:30:45.220
is start to understand that
network of other vessels

00:30:45.220 --> 00:30:47.290
that were involved in
that and transshipped

00:30:47.290 --> 00:30:50.650
their catch onto this boat,
because this boat itself wasn't

00:30:50.650 --> 00:30:53.830
the one that was doing
all of that fishing.

00:30:53.830 --> 00:30:56.080
DAVID KROODMSA: So I think
that's a really good point,

00:30:56.080 --> 00:30:57.970
that the fishing was
actually happening elsewhere

00:30:57.970 --> 00:30:58.600
in the ocean.

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:01.450
And really what's
exciting this is

00:31:01.450 --> 00:31:04.050
that the high seas, the ocean,
is kind of this last frontier

00:31:04.050 --> 00:31:07.800
and this area that's synonymous
with things that you can't tell

00:31:07.800 --> 00:31:09.152
is happening there.

00:31:09.152 --> 00:31:10.860
And so what happens
is that we over-fish,

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:12.193
and all those things are caught.

00:31:12.193 --> 00:31:15.600
And all these dangerous
species are being caught.

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.922
And it's a huge
management problem.

00:31:17.922 --> 00:31:20.130
And once you can see what's
actually happening there,

00:31:20.130 --> 00:31:22.350
people can start to take
action and do something.

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:23.780
And so I think that--

00:31:23.780 --> 00:31:25.980
it's a place where I
think it's really--

00:31:25.980 --> 00:31:27.900
like new data is
really exciting,

00:31:27.900 --> 00:31:29.814
because it makes a difference.

00:31:29.814 --> 00:31:31.480
BRIAN SULLIVAN: This
is another example.

00:31:31.480 --> 00:31:33.040
This is the Sanchi.

00:31:33.040 --> 00:31:37.510
This was an oil tanker that
crashed with another vessel

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:39.560
out in the middle
of the Asia-Pacific.

00:31:39.560 --> 00:31:42.055
And because it had so much oil
and other toxic [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:42.055 --> 00:31:46.810
on it, it ignited, and it
burned for well over a week

00:31:46.810 --> 00:31:48.790
in the middle of the high seas.

00:31:48.790 --> 00:31:51.160
And so this was an
oil spill that's

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:53.230
on par with a lot of
the other famous ones

00:31:53.230 --> 00:31:54.910
that people have
heard about in the US.

00:31:54.910 --> 00:31:57.530
But because it's happening out
in the middle of the ocean,

00:31:57.530 --> 00:31:59.800
it's not really getting
any coverage the same way.

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:01.240
And it's really,
really difficult

00:32:01.240 --> 00:32:03.730
to estimate what the
impact this will have

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:06.090
as toxins are
leaking out all over.

00:32:06.090 --> 00:32:09.040
And because we've made all
of our data completely open,

00:32:09.040 --> 00:32:11.140
Reuters has gone
on, and they started

00:32:11.140 --> 00:32:15.550
modeling what the effects of
that tanker are going to be.

00:32:15.550 --> 00:32:17.350
And so they built
out an area that

00:32:17.350 --> 00:32:19.302
looked at where the oil
was going to spread.

00:32:19.302 --> 00:32:21.760
And then, they brought in all
the Global Fishing Watch data

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:24.910
as well and were able to
say over that same two

00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:27.430
or three-week period
that they were able to--

00:32:27.430 --> 00:32:29.554
actually, I think it was
about a three-month period

00:32:29.554 --> 00:32:31.780
that they modeled out where
the oil was able to go,

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:33.370
they could see which
fishing grounds

00:32:33.370 --> 00:32:35.170
were going to be affected.

00:32:35.170 --> 00:32:37.750
And as we look at other ways
of integrating Global Fishing

00:32:37.750 --> 00:32:39.820
Watch data, there's
a huge opportunity

00:32:39.820 --> 00:32:43.090
to work with local markets
so that you could actually

00:32:43.090 --> 00:32:46.060
see where your fish was caught
and whether it may or may not

00:32:46.060 --> 00:32:47.950
have been in the
vicinity of some

00:32:47.950 --> 00:32:50.800
of these natural disasters
or man-made disasters

00:32:50.800 --> 00:32:54.030
as they're happening.

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:57.780
There's another example here
that's, I think, very telling.

00:32:57.780 --> 00:33:01.080
Traditionally, when new
marine protected areas

00:33:01.080 --> 00:33:03.300
wanted to be created,
there is often

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:07.320
pushback from local
industry and other areas.

00:33:07.320 --> 00:33:08.782
And what's happening
here is there

00:33:08.782 --> 00:33:10.740
is a new marine protected
area they were trying

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:15.030
to create in Mexico, and that's
this little red box in here

00:33:15.030 --> 00:33:16.090
that they tried to do.

00:33:16.090 --> 00:33:18.330
And I'll try to do this live.

00:33:18.330 --> 00:33:19.980
We'll see if it
cooperates this time.

00:33:27.330 --> 00:33:28.990
One more time.

00:33:28.990 --> 00:33:33.500
And so when they went in,
the industry had said,

00:33:33.500 --> 00:33:35.650
look, if you close
this area to fishing,

00:33:35.650 --> 00:33:37.120
we'll lose job security.

00:33:37.120 --> 00:33:40.540
We'll lose the fish that
we need to feed the country

00:33:40.540 --> 00:33:42.040
and everything else.

00:33:42.040 --> 00:33:44.080
And traditionally, this
has been very one-sided,

00:33:44.080 --> 00:33:46.090
because industry has
had this information,

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:49.990
but governments and NGOs and
other public interest groups

00:33:49.990 --> 00:33:51.670
have not had access to this.

00:33:51.670 --> 00:33:53.192
With Global Fishing
Watch, they were

00:33:53.192 --> 00:33:57.370
able to say, OK, here is
everywhere the Mexican fishing

00:33:57.370 --> 00:33:59.290
vessels are fishing.

00:33:59.290 --> 00:34:03.640
And what we're able to see is
that that little red area there

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:06.910
is less than 4%
of the total fish

00:34:06.910 --> 00:34:09.920
and the total profits that
are being collected by this.

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:13.397
And a lot of the work is being
done out in the high seas.

00:34:13.397 --> 00:34:14.980
DAVID KROODMSA: And
I just want to say

00:34:14.980 --> 00:34:17.610
it's really great, because
these arguments usually--

00:34:17.610 --> 00:34:19.179
they can start
having conversations

00:34:19.179 --> 00:34:21.400
about actual numbers and
things once you bring this

00:34:21.400 --> 00:34:21.941
to the table.

00:34:21.941 --> 00:34:24.730
Because before, it's like people
come to the table with like,

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:27.105
no, marine protected areas
are going destroy our business

00:34:27.105 --> 00:34:28.690
or marine protected
areas are great,

00:34:28.690 --> 00:34:30.190
and they're going
to save the ocean.

00:34:30.190 --> 00:34:32.170
And now, we can actually
say, we're actually to save--

00:34:32.170 --> 00:34:33.940
they're going to prevent
this percentage of fishing

00:34:33.940 --> 00:34:34.939
and save this much area.

00:34:34.939 --> 00:34:37.449
And then, that allows you to
actually make progress and have

00:34:37.449 --> 00:34:38.804
real conversations.

00:34:38.804 --> 00:34:40.179
BRIAN SULLIVAN:
And in that case,

00:34:40.179 --> 00:34:42.040
National Geographic
and Pristine Seas

00:34:42.040 --> 00:34:44.710
has a program where they
work with local governments

00:34:44.710 --> 00:34:48.639
to enable them to have access to
this other type of information

00:34:48.639 --> 00:34:50.949
and figure out, from a
scientific perspective, where

00:34:50.949 --> 00:34:52.210
these policies can be done.

00:34:52.210 --> 00:34:53.860
And in the last
year or two, they

00:34:53.860 --> 00:34:57.220
have actually created new five
marine protected areas using

00:34:57.220 --> 00:34:58.900
data from Global Fishing Watch.

00:34:58.900 --> 00:35:01.930
Some of these areas are
larger than California,

00:35:01.930 --> 00:35:04.060
twice the size of Germany,
and we're seeing this

00:35:04.060 --> 00:35:05.060
all over the world now.

00:35:05.060 --> 00:35:07.342
And we're really
excited about it.

00:35:07.342 --> 00:35:09.300
So one of the things
that's exciting about this

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:11.850
is that Global Fishing Watch
would not have been possible

00:35:11.850 --> 00:35:13.530
five years ago.

00:35:13.530 --> 00:35:17.580
Satellite data didn't have the
global coverage that we need.

00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:19.980
From a regulatory perspective,
a lot of these vessels

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:21.750
did not used to
publicly broadcast,

00:35:21.750 --> 00:35:23.880
and we're seeing more and
more pressure from that.

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:26.550
But then finally, the
computing power was never there

00:35:26.550 --> 00:35:31.050
to handle massive, massive
amounts of this data

00:35:31.050 --> 00:35:33.120
and be able to scale
it over an area

00:35:33.120 --> 00:35:36.990
that you didn't have to be a
hardcore computer programmer

00:35:36.990 --> 00:35:39.280
to start to be able to
tease out that information

00:35:39.280 --> 00:35:42.030
and be able to visualize
everything in a simple web

00:35:42.030 --> 00:35:44.850
page that's free and public
and anybody in the world

00:35:44.850 --> 00:35:46.430
can go in and see that.

00:35:46.430 --> 00:35:48.150
And so we're really
excited by the fact

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:51.000
that five years ago,
this wasn't possible.

00:35:51.000 --> 00:35:53.200
It's just starting
to be possible now.

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:54.930
And it keeps
growing and growing.

00:35:54.930 --> 00:35:56.638
And a lot of the things
that you've seen,

00:35:56.638 --> 00:36:00.300
we've done all just off of the
equivalent of a GPS broadcast.

00:36:00.300 --> 00:36:02.190
But now the next wave
is going to be looking

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:05.280
at all kinds of satellite
imagery and radar

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:08.550
and other ones to get an even
bigger sense of what extent

00:36:08.550 --> 00:36:10.800
we're fishing our
oceans, so we can better

00:36:10.800 --> 00:36:12.482
manage them for the future.

00:36:12.482 --> 00:36:13.940
So thanks very
much, and I think we

00:36:13.940 --> 00:36:15.190
have questions coming shortly.

00:36:43.125 --> 00:36:45.189
All right, well welcome back.

00:36:45.189 --> 00:36:47.480
All right, so we have some
questions from the audience.

00:36:47.480 --> 00:36:49.440
Some of these are
often the questions

00:36:49.440 --> 00:36:51.140
we frequently get
asked, and there's

00:36:51.140 --> 00:36:52.880
a couple new ones in here too.

00:36:52.880 --> 00:36:55.160
So all right, the first
question is, why can't boats

00:36:55.160 --> 00:36:57.920
just turn off their AIS,
and you'll never see them?

00:36:57.920 --> 00:36:59.970
DAVID KROODMSA: They can.

00:36:59.970 --> 00:37:01.160
That's the answer.

00:37:01.160 --> 00:37:03.800
But the trick is, you can
see when they turn it off,

00:37:03.800 --> 00:37:05.020
if they're away from shore.

00:37:05.020 --> 00:37:06.952
And so you can-- and
that's suspicious,

00:37:06.952 --> 00:37:07.910
so we can highlight it.

00:37:07.910 --> 00:37:09.326
And we've done
some analyses where

00:37:09.326 --> 00:37:11.840
we compare the number of days
at sea for certain fleets

00:37:11.840 --> 00:37:14.420
with official statistics,
and it does quite well.

00:37:14.420 --> 00:37:16.942
So this is a problem in some
specific parts of the world

00:37:16.942 --> 00:37:18.650
that we have to do
more investigation on.

00:37:18.650 --> 00:37:21.860
But for most of the data set,
it is not a huge problem.

00:37:21.860 --> 00:37:23.525
BRIAN SULLIVAN: OK.

00:37:23.525 --> 00:37:27.250
And actually, I think Oceana,
another nonprofit that we've

00:37:27.250 --> 00:37:29.800
worked with, recently
released a report where

00:37:29.800 --> 00:37:31.880
they looked across the globe.

00:37:31.880 --> 00:37:34.510
And they said, show
me examples of boats

00:37:34.510 --> 00:37:37.720
that have kind of consistently
been turning off their AIS just

00:37:37.720 --> 00:37:40.390
as they're approaching
protected areas

00:37:40.390 --> 00:37:42.326
or other exclusive
economic zones.

00:37:42.326 --> 00:37:44.200
And we're not saying
what's legal or illegal,

00:37:44.200 --> 00:37:45.880
but we're putting
the data out there

00:37:45.880 --> 00:37:47.170
to start having conversations.

00:37:47.170 --> 00:37:48.670
And we think that's
really the value

00:37:48.670 --> 00:37:49.420
of transparent information.

00:37:49.420 --> 00:37:50.710
DAVID KROODMSA: And
to be clear, there

00:37:50.710 --> 00:37:52.480
are places where
this is an issue that

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:56.105
requires that type of attention
that Oceana has brought to.

00:37:56.105 --> 00:37:57.480
BRIAN SULLIVAN:
OK, so do you see

00:37:57.480 --> 00:37:59.610
any difference between
running the machine learning

00:37:59.610 --> 00:38:02.610
models and virtual machines
versus in Cloud ML?

00:38:02.610 --> 00:38:03.990
And did you get any uplift?

00:38:03.990 --> 00:38:06.460
And have you played
with GPUs or TPUs?

00:38:06.460 --> 00:38:09.140
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah,
those are good questions.

00:38:09.140 --> 00:38:11.330
Cloud ML is really nice
because of the auto-scaling.

00:38:11.330 --> 00:38:12.540
So you don't need
to officially--

00:38:12.540 --> 00:38:14.998
you don't have to allocate all
of your machines beforehand.

00:38:14.998 --> 00:38:17.920
And it's allowed us to do
things much more efficiently.

00:38:17.920 --> 00:38:21.510
We have not been able to use
TPUs, Tensor Processing Units,

00:38:21.510 --> 00:38:25.410
but we have moved to GPUs,
which have about 10 to 20 times

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:26.580
more efficient--

00:38:26.580 --> 00:38:32.142
faster than doing our model
on CPUs, which is really nice.

00:38:32.142 --> 00:38:33.100
Makes a big difference.

00:38:33.100 --> 00:38:33.450
BRIAN SULLIVAN:
And I think what's

00:38:33.450 --> 00:38:35.670
happened is not only
is it so much faster,

00:38:35.670 --> 00:38:37.740
but then it also
decreases your costs.

00:38:37.740 --> 00:38:40.005
Because you're not running
the machines for as long as

00:38:40.005 --> 00:38:40.522
you need.

00:38:40.522 --> 00:38:41.397
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah.

00:38:41.397 --> 00:38:43.813
BRIAN SULLIVAN: All right, so
it sounds like you're basing

00:38:43.813 --> 00:38:45.210
this whole project off of AIS--

00:38:45.210 --> 00:38:47.820
other types of tracking
information you can use?

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:50.470
So I think we actually touched
on that a little bit in there.

00:38:50.470 --> 00:38:52.470
So governments like
Indonesia are coming forward

00:38:52.470 --> 00:38:56.160
and providing their own
vessel monitoring system data.

00:38:56.160 --> 00:38:59.040
And so I was actually--
in June of last year

00:38:59.040 --> 00:39:02.730
at the UN Oceans event,
we stood up with Ibu Susi,

00:39:02.730 --> 00:39:05.160
and we launched all the
Indonesian data in there

00:39:05.160 --> 00:39:05.847
as well.

00:39:05.847 --> 00:39:08.430
And since then, we've actually
built out a team of about three

00:39:08.430 --> 00:39:10.860
or four people that are
in Indonesia working

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:13.590
with the local government
to provide them

00:39:13.590 --> 00:39:16.380
access to these tools and build
the type of custom reports

00:39:16.380 --> 00:39:18.654
and tools that they need
to push these further.

00:39:18.654 --> 00:39:20.820
And we also talked a little
bit about how we're just

00:39:20.820 --> 00:39:23.670
starting to use a lot more
satellite imagery and radar

00:39:23.670 --> 00:39:26.550
and other areas to measure
what we call the dark fleet,

00:39:26.550 --> 00:39:28.560
or vessels that
aren't broadcasting.

00:39:28.560 --> 00:39:30.450
DAVID KROODMSA: And
we're actually soon going

00:39:30.450 --> 00:39:32.250
to be adding to Global Fishing
Watch a layer where you can--

00:39:32.250 --> 00:39:33.930
there's another
thing called VIIRS,

00:39:33.930 --> 00:39:37.170
which is a satellite from NOAA,
that looks at night lights.

00:39:37.170 --> 00:39:40.222
And so squid fishing vessels
fish with really bright lights,

00:39:40.222 --> 00:39:42.180
and so these satellites
can actually see those.

00:39:42.180 --> 00:39:44.517
And we're starting to match
those up to fishing vessels.

00:39:44.517 --> 00:39:45.600
And we'll be able to see--

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:47.183
you'll be able see
that soon in Global

00:39:47.183 --> 00:39:50.410
Fishing Watch, that
layer of that satellite.

00:39:50.410 --> 00:39:52.410
And so you'll be able to
see those night lights.

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:53.951
And it is really
interesting, there's

00:39:53.951 --> 00:39:56.084
some places where there's
boats that don't have AIS

00:39:56.084 --> 00:39:57.000
but are fishing squid.

00:39:57.000 --> 00:40:00.300
And you can see that
now in this map.

00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:02.647
BRIAN SULLIVAN: How can
people get access to the data?

00:40:02.647 --> 00:40:04.230
DAVID KROODMSA:
Globalfishingwatch.org

00:40:04.230 --> 00:40:06.767
and globalfishingwatch.io.

00:40:06.767 --> 00:40:08.350
BRIAN SULLIVAN: All
right, that works.

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:08.974
Just Google it.

00:40:08.974 --> 00:40:11.070
DAVID KROODMSA: Just
Google it, yeah.

00:40:11.070 --> 00:40:14.940
So the raw AIS tracks, we
can't release all of those,

00:40:14.940 --> 00:40:17.505
because some of those
are proprietary.

00:40:17.505 --> 00:40:21.150
But we have high-resolution
gridded fishing effort

00:40:21.150 --> 00:40:23.930
by vessel and--

00:40:23.930 --> 00:40:25.140
that you can-- by day.

00:40:25.140 --> 00:40:26.820
So you can really
kind of dig down

00:40:26.820 --> 00:40:28.254
to the results of these models.

00:40:28.254 --> 00:40:29.670
BRIAN SULLIVAN:
And I think you've

00:40:29.670 --> 00:40:33.410
made it available in CSVs,
in BigQuery directly,

00:40:33.410 --> 00:40:34.410
so you can play with it.

00:40:34.410 --> 00:40:37.030
It's also been rasterized and
available on Earth Engine.

00:40:37.030 --> 00:40:39.450
So if you want to bring it
in along with sea surface

00:40:39.450 --> 00:40:41.927
temperature, or depth
and others like that,

00:40:41.927 --> 00:40:43.260
it's all in one package already.

00:40:43.260 --> 00:40:45.468
DAVID KROODMSA: And on
globalfishingwatch.io, there's

00:40:45.468 --> 00:40:47.780
sample code and blog posts
to describe each of those,

00:40:47.780 --> 00:40:49.860
so BigQuery and Earth
Engine, how to access them.

00:40:49.860 --> 00:40:51.354
BRIAN SULLIVAN: OK.

00:40:51.354 --> 00:40:52.770
DAVID KROODMSA: I
highly recommend

00:40:52.770 --> 00:40:54.000
using those over the CSVs.

00:40:54.000 --> 00:40:57.450
Because the CSVs just give
you the 25-gigabyte file,

00:40:57.450 --> 00:40:59.380
and it's much
harder to deal with.

00:40:59.380 --> 00:41:01.380
BRIAN SULLIVAN: And then
finally, in the last 30

00:41:01.380 --> 00:41:03.090
seconds, what's wrong?

00:41:03.090 --> 00:41:04.516
Where are the holes?

00:41:04.516 --> 00:41:05.640
Where do you lack coverage?

00:41:05.640 --> 00:41:06.240
DAVID KROODMSA: Oh, nowhere.

00:41:06.240 --> 00:41:06.800
It's perfect.

00:41:06.800 --> 00:41:08.910
[CHUCKLING]

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:11.130
So the main issue is that
not all boats have AIS,

00:41:11.130 --> 00:41:14.730
and it varies by region.

00:41:14.730 --> 00:41:17.640
We dove into this on a
recent science publication.

00:41:17.640 --> 00:41:20.790
You can look in the
supplementary materials.

00:41:20.790 --> 00:41:23.490
There's a large table
segged by country.

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:25.445
But we have documented
this quite extensively.

00:41:25.445 --> 00:41:27.570
There's also some regions
where AIS coverage is not

00:41:27.570 --> 00:41:28.530
quite as good.

00:41:28.530 --> 00:41:32.184
But by and large, it's a
problem that we can address.

00:41:32.184 --> 00:41:33.100
BRIAN SULLIVAN: Great.

00:41:33.100 --> 00:41:33.830
DAVID KROODMSA: Great.

00:41:33.830 --> 00:41:34.830
BRIAN SULLIVAN: All right,
well thanks so much.

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:36.120
It was a pleasure today.

00:41:36.120 --> 00:41:38.250
And I think there's
another one coming next.

00:41:38.250 --> 00:41:39.150
DAVID KROODMSA: Yeah.

00:41:39.150 --> 00:41:42.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

